  "query_preview": "..\nA couple of prominent features have led to the inclusion of \nscattered showers and isolated thunderstorms for tonight. An upper- \nlevel low is centered W of Guam, and is expected to remain quasi- \nstationary, keeping upper-level divergence favorable for convection \nat least through tonight. A weak trade-wind trough extending from the\nSE will continue its westward motion. However, incoming Precipitable\nWater (PWAT) values of 2.0 inches and the previously mentioned \nupper-level low will keep the Marianas in a convectively-prone \nweather pattern. As for Monday, the relatively dry trade-wind regime \nwill continue, with island heating leading to showers building up \nover leeward portions of the islands before being advected offshore \nby the trade winds. Invest 92W is of sub-low status according to \nJTWC. Consequently, forecaster confidence is low regarding potential \nimpacts to the Marianas in the latter half of the week. Therefore, \nProbability of Precipitation (POP) will remain at 30 percent until \nthere is increased certainty regarding Invest 92W (refer to the \nTropical Systems section below).",
